# Every file is stored LF in the repository. What differs is what lands in the
# working tree, and that follows whichever compiler is fussiest about it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The C sources check out CRLF, because DOS is the primary target and Turbo C
# 2.01 miscompiles a source file with Unix line endings: it emits a nearly
# empty object, reports nothing at all, and the failure surfaces much later as
# an undefined _main at link time. Measured here before this rule existed.
#
# The other four compilers read either ending, and so does every modern one, so
# this costs the secondary hosts nothing.
#
# eol=crlf changes the checkout only. The blobs stay LF, so diffs, blame and
# review are unaffected, and anything parsing a source file in the working tree
# wants tr -d '\r'.
*.c text eol=crlf
*.h text eol=crlf

# What DOS parses: COMMAND.COM wants CRLF and mis-parses a lone LF, and
# MSCBUILD.BAT and TCBUILD.BAT are builds that run there. Both spellings of the
# extension, because pattern matching is only case insensitive on a case
# insensitive filesystem and the files are MSCBUILD.BAT and TCBUILD.BAT.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.BAT text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# The DOS/32A licence, shipped in the DOS archive and read there.
tools/dos32a-license.txt text eol=crlf

# Screenshots are binary; text=auto above would otherwise guess, and a mangled
# PNG is not obvious until somebody opens the README.
*.png binary
